48057	48175	The Sheffield Rally

The campaign's most notorious moment came with Labour's rally at Sheffield Arena on 1 April 1992.
48176	48324	The event, in preparation for eighteen months and costing £100,000, was attended by 10,000 Labour Party members including the entire shadow cabinet.
48325	48489	The rally was the brainchild of strategist Philip Gould, modelled on American presidential conventions with sound and light performances and celebrity endorsements.
48490	48682	Kinnock was flown in by helicopter and the shadow cabinet paraded through the crowd to the stage, being introduced with titles such as "The next Home Secretary" and "The next Prime Minister".

48682	48809	The rally culminated with an emotional Kinnock taking the podium and shouting what was generally reported as "We're all right!"
48810	48883	four times, though Kinnock later claimed he had shouted "Well all right!"
48884	48924	in the manner of a rock and roll singer.
